madam chief justice madam deputy chief  these judges at kenya's supreme court  have a difficult decision to make if we  look at the number of the polling  stations they've been hearing a  presidential election case that has been  disputed  president-elect william ruto was  declared winner of last month's poll his  opponent rylo dinger came second  odinga says the election was rigged  at the heart of the case is a role  played by an increasingly divided  electoral commission  four of its commissioners have rejected  the results  the others including the chair of the  commission wafula chabukati  their colleagues of trying to force a  runoff to see them being divided on  an issue that the entire of the  commission  was managing is really surprising and is  very  scary even to some extent because we are  talking about  a country which has nearly gone to war  because of elections this is not the  first time that kenya is going through a  hotly contested judicial process after a  national poll  the presidential election was nullified  in 2017 for the first time in africa  judges said the poll was murdered by  irregularities some kenyans say this  time  the stakes are much higher  opinion outside the courtroom is divided  misinformation is rife and those who are  trying to get a sense of what is  happening say they are overwhelmed  we're looking at twitter on facebook on  whatsapp on instagram tick tock all  these platforms were awash with a lot of  false information and we see this  because the institutions that are  charged with giving kenyans regular  updates or credible information were  taking a step back  a group calling itself the people's  parliament has also been monitoring  what's going on in street corners  but even they say they are polarized  their debates are based on which  political side they support one thing is  clear  we want our courts  to dispense nothing but justice  kama supreme court in under could  nullify if the supreme court is going to  nullify i am telling william ruto we are  ready we shall vote you in again  many kenyans say they just want to know  the truth no matter which way the court  rules  catherine soy al jazeera nairobi
 

